## Included content<a id="content"></a>

|        Module         | What it does |
| --------------------- | ------------ |
{# skeleton-variant: http-readme-3 #}
| `fixture`             | Creates, changes or deletes one record |
| `fixture_info`        | Reads one record without changing it |
| `fixture_nested`      | Creates, changes or deletes a record with nested settings |
| `fixture_nested_info` | Reads that record without changing it |
{# end skeleton-variant #}

Each module documents its options, return values and own rules:

```sh
ansible-doc {{ namespace }}.{{ collection_name }}.fixture
```


## Requirements<a id="requirements"></a>

You prepare the following before the first task:

- A running installation of the product whose API the Ansible control node, or
  another host you delegate to, can reach.
- An API key and secret for the automation, stored in Ansible Vault or another
  secret store.
- Trust in the API's TLS certificate on the host that runs the modules.
- ansible-core {{ min_ansible_version | default('2.19.0') }} or newer on the
  control node, and Python 3.9 or newer on the host that runs the modules.
- FIXME List the product's other requirements, such as the privileges of the
  automation account.

{# skeleton-variant: http-readme-4 #}
{# end skeleton-variant #}
### Dependencies<a id="dependencies"></a>

See `dependencies` in [`galaxy.yml`](./galaxy.yml).

`ansible-galaxy collection install` installs these collections together with
this one. The fixture uses `foundata.utils >=1.0.0`. Python packages the
modules need beyond ansible-core are listed in
[`requirements.txt`](./requirements.txt); the fixture needs none.


## Getting started<a id="getting-started"></a>

{# skeleton-variant: http-readme-5 #}
The modules talk to the API over HTTPS, so they run on the control node
(`connection: local`) and not on the product's host. Set the connection options
once for the whole play with `module_defaults`:
{# end skeleton-variant #}

```yaml
---
- name: {{ ('Configure ' ~ product | default('Example Product')) | to_json(ensure_ascii=False) }}
  hosts: "product_api"
  connection: "local"
  gather_facts: false
  become: false
  module_defaults:
    group/{{ namespace }}.{{ collection_name }}.api:
      url: "{% raw %}{{ product_url }}{% endraw %}"
      api_key: "{% raw %}{{ product_api_key }}{% endraw %}"
      api_secret: "{% raw %}{{ product_api_secret }}{% endraw %}"
  tasks:
{# skeleton-variant: http-readme-6 #}
    - name: "Save the record"
      {{ namespace }}.{{ collection_name }}.fixture:
        name: "example"
        value: "ready"
      register: "__record"
{# end skeleton-variant #}
```

{# skeleton-variant: http-readme-7 #}
The first run creates the record and reports `changed: true`. A second run
finds it as declared and reports `changed: false`. In both cases the result
holds the record as the product stores it, without secrets:

```yaml
__record:
  changed: true
  changed_fields: []
  verified: "observed"
  fixture:
    name: "example"
    id: 7
    value: "ready"
    # further public fields of the record
```

`verified: observed` means the module read the record back after writing it.
[How the modules behave](#module-behavior) explains the other values.
{# end skeleton-variant #}


