## How the modules behave<a id="module-behavior"></a>

All modules of the collection follow the same rules. The table gives each rule
in one line; the sections below add the details you need when a run surprises
you.

|      Topic       | Rule |
| ---------------- | ---- |
| Managed options  | A module changes only the options you set. |
{# skeleton-variant: http-readme-10 #}
| Finding a record | A record is found by its `name`; a changed name is another record. |
| Taking effect    | A write is active as soon as the task ends. |
{# end skeleton-variant #}
| Check mode       | Check mode reads and predicts; it never writes. |
| Failures         | A failed task says whether it changed anything. |
{# skeleton-variant: http-readme-11 #}
| Lost replies     | A module never sends a creation twice. |
{# end skeleton-variant #}
| Product versions | An untested product series warns; a known-bad build fails before writing. |

### What a module manages<a id="behavior-ownership"></a>

A module changes only the options you set in the task. An option you leave out,
or set to `null`, keeps whatever value the product has. To clear a field, set
it to an empty value, such as `""` or `[]`.

{# skeleton-variant: http-readme-12 #}
A password is write-only: the API never returns it, so a module cannot tell
whether it changed. The module therefore sets it when it creates the record and
leaves it alone afterwards, unless you ask for a rotation with
`update_password: always`. The `access_secret` is different, because the API
can read it: the module compares it on every run and changes it when it
differs. An empty string clears it. Neither value appears in a result or a
diff.

Deleting a record that another record still refers to fails. Delete or change
the referring record first.
{# end skeleton-variant #}

Two writers on the same endpoint can overwrite each other. Let one play at a
time write to an installation. This includes inventory hosts that are aliases
of the same endpoint.

### How a module finds a record<a id="behavior-identity"></a>

{# skeleton-variant: http-readme-13 #}
A module finds a record by its `name`, ignoring case. The product assigns the
numeric `id`; you never pass it. Changing `name` in a task therefore does not
rename a record: it creates a second one.

A parent you name in `parent` must exist exactly once. The module looks it up
before it writes anything and fails with `error_category: reference` when no
parent or more than one parent has that name.
{# end skeleton-variant #}

### When a change takes effect<a id="behavior-activation"></a>

{# skeleton-variant: http-readme-14 #}
A write through the REST API is active immediately. The collection has no
separate apply step.
{# end skeleton-variant #}

{# skeleton-variant: http-readme-15 #}
{# end skeleton-variant #}
### Check mode and diff mode<a id="behavior-check-diff"></a>

{# skeleton-variant: http-readme-16 #}
In check mode (`--check`) a module reads the current state and predicts the
result, but sends no write. The `verified` value in the result tells you what
the reported state is based on:

- `observed`: the module read this state from the product. Nothing would
  change, or the module ran outside check mode and read the record back.
- `predicted`: check mode skipped a write. The state shown is what the module
  expects after it. A predicted new record has no `id`, because the product
  has not assigned one.
- `unverifiable`: the module wrote a write-only password, which it cannot read
  back.

The `_info` modules always report observed state.

With `--diff`, the result shows the managed options before and after, without
secrets.
{# end skeleton-variant #}

### What a failed task reports<a id="behavior-failures"></a>

A failed task carries these values next to `msg`, so that a play or a person
can decide what to do:

|             Value              | Meaning |
| ------------------------------ | ------- |
| `error_category`, `error_code` | What kind of failure it was, as fixed words |
| `http_status`, `operation`     | The API's status code and the step that failed |
| `name`                         | The record the task addressed |
| `changed`, `outcome`           | Whether the product changed: `unchanged`, `changed` or `unknown` |

{# skeleton-variant: http-readme-17 #}
`outcome: unknown` means the module could not find out, for example because
the connection broke after it sent a password. Check the record before you run
the task again.
{# end skeleton-variant #}

When the reply to a write is lost, the module reads the record back by its name
to see whether the write arrived. It never sends a creation a second time on
its own.

Results and error messages never contain secrets, also not secrets that the API
returns in a listing.

### Product versions<a id="behavior-versions"></a>

